"""English: extended material for Stage 2 · Experimental design (part A)."""
from . import LE

LE("rancangan-acak-lengkap",
   [("Estimators, standard errors, and intervals for treatment means",
     [r"By least squares, the estimator of the \(i\)-th treatment mean is its sample mean, and \(\sigma^2\) is estimated by the MSE, which pools the variation within all treatments. Because the MSE uses all the error degrees of freedom, the standard error of a treatment mean is more stable than a standard deviation computed from one treatment alone.",
      r"The standard error of a difference between two means is the basis of LSD, HSD, and confidence intervals for differences. With unequal replication, the standard error of a difference is different for each pair."]),
    ("Expected mean squares and why the F test works",
     [r"The F test is valid because of the expected values of the two mean squares. The MSE always estimates \(\sigma^2\). The MSTR estimates \(\sigma^2\) plus a term proportional to the variation among treatment effects. When \(H_0\) is true that term is zero, so both mean squares estimate the same thing and their ratio hovers around 1; when \(H_0\) is false, F tends to exceed 1.",
      r"Under the random model, that term is replaced by \(r\sigma^2_\tau\) and the same test tests \(H_0:\sigma^2_\tau=0\). This is the general pattern used in every design: the denominator of F is the mean square whose expected value equals the numerator's when \(H_0\) is true."]),
    ("CRD with subsampling",
     [r"Often one experimental unit is measured several times, for example three plants per pot. The model then has two sources of variation: experimental error (between pots within a treatment) and sampling error (between plants within a pot). Treatments must be tested against the experimental error, not the sampling error; using the sampling error is pseudoreplication.",
      r"The expected mean squares show how much is gained by adding subsamples rather than experimental units: increasing \(s\) only shrinks the term \(\sigma^2_\delta/s\), while increasing \(r\) shrinks the whole standard error."]),
    ("Power of the F test and the number of replicates",
     [r"When \(H_0\) is false, the F statistic follows a noncentral F distribution with noncentrality parameter \(\lambda\). Power is the probability that the noncentral F exceeds the critical value of the central F. Power used to be read from operating characteristic curves using \(\phi=\sqrt{\lambda/t}\); today it is computed directly with software.",
      r"A practical approach: choose the largest difference between means \(D\) that should be detected. The configuration hardest to detect puts two treatments at the extremes and the rest in the middle, giving \(\sum\tau_i^2=D^2/2\)."]),
    ("Reporting a CRD",
     [r"A good report contains: the full ANOVA table (source, df, SS, MS, F, P), the CV, treatment means with standard errors and post-hoc letters, the name and level of the post-hoc test, and the results of assumption checks. For quantitative treatments, present a response curve (regression or polynomial contrasts), not letters.",
      r"In agricultural field trials a CV below about 20% is generally considered acceptable, and in the laboratory it is usually lower; but the CV depends on the trait measured, so compare it with similar experiments rather than a single fixed number."])],
   [(r"In a CRD with MSE \(=6\), treatment A has 4 replicates and B has 6. Compute the standard error of the difference between the means of A and B.",
     r"\(SE=\sqrt{6(1/4+1/6)}=\sqrt{6(0.4167)}=\sqrt{2.5}=1.58\)."),
    (r"In a CRD with subsampling, why is the treatment F computed with the experimental-error mean square as the denominator?",
     r"Because when \(H_0\) is true, \(E(MSTR)=\sigma^2_\delta+s\sigma^2_\varepsilon\), the same as the expected experimental-error mean square. The sampling-error mean square estimates only \(\sigma^2_\delta\), which is too small, so F would be too large."),
    (r"The variance component between pots is \(\sigma^2_\varepsilon=4\) and between plants within a pot \(\sigma^2_\delta=8\). Compare the variance of a treatment mean for (a) \(r=4\), \(s=2\) and (b) \(r=2\), \(s=4\).",
     r"(a) \(4/4+8/8=2.0\). (b) \(4/2+8/8=3.0\). With the same number of plants (8), adding pots is more effective than adding plants per pot."),
    (r"Four treatments, \(r=5\), \(\sigma=2\). What is the minimum noncentrality parameter if the largest difference to detect is \(D=3\)?",
     r"\(\lambda_{\min}=rD^2/(2\sigma^2)=5(9)/(2\times4)=5.625\)."),
    (r"A CRD gives MSE \(=2.25\) and a grand mean of 30. What is the CV, and what does it mean?",
     r"\(CV=\sqrt{2.25}/30\times100\%=1.5/30\times100\%=5\%\). The experimental error is small relative to the mean; the experiment is fairly precise.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: CRD with unequal replication",
                soal="Three growing media (A, B, C) were tested, but some pots were damaged so replication is 4, 3, and 5. Seedling height (cm) is shown in the table. Build the ANOVA and compare the means.",
                data=[["Medium", "Replicates", "Data", "Total", "Mean"], ["A", "4", "12, 14, 13, 15", "54", "13.5"], ["B", "3", "16, 18, 17", "51", "17.0"], ["C", "5", "11, 10, 12, 13, 9", "55", "11.0"], ["Total", "12", "", "160", "13.33"]],
                langkah=["Correction factor and total SS (sum of squares of all data 2,218).",
                         "Treatment SS uses each treatment's own number of replicates as divisor.",
                         "Error SS, mean squares, and F with df 2 and 9.",
                         "The standard error of a difference differs for each pair.",
                         "5% LSD for each pair with t(0.025; 9) = 2.262."],
                simpul="Medium has a highly significant effect (F = 17.9; P = 0.0007; CV = 10.3%). B − A = 3.5 > 2.37, A − C = 2.5 > 2.09, and B − C = 6.0 > 2.27, so all three differ significantly: B > A > C."))

LE("rancangan-acak-kelompok",
   [("Expected mean squares and the test for blocks",
     [r"In an RCBD, the MSE estimates \(\sigma^2\) and the MSTR estimates \(\sigma^2+b\sum\tau_j^2/(t-1)\), so treatments are tested with F = MSTR/MSE. The ratio MSB/MSE is often printed too, but it is not a valid test for blocks: blocks are not randomised, and randomisation is restricted within blocks. Read the ratio only as a sign of whether blocking worked (a value well above 1 means the blocks really differ).",
      r"The RCBD model assumes no block × treatment interaction; any interaction present goes into the error and inflates the MSE."]),
    ("Deriving Yates' missing-value formula",
     [r"When one observation is missing, Yates proposed filling it with the value \(x\) that minimises the error SS. Differentiating SSE with respect to \(x\) and setting it to zero gives the formula below, where \(B\), \(T\), and \(G\) are the block, treatment, and grand totals without the missing value.",
      r"The estimated value adds no information: the error and total df are each reduced by one. The treatment SS is also slightly inflated; its bias correction is subtracted from SSTR. Modern software avoids this filling by analysing the unbalanced data through general least squares or a mixed model."]),
    ("Additivity and Tukey's test",
     [r"Because an RCBD without replication within cells cannot separate interaction from error, additivity is checked with Tukey's one-degree-of-freedom test. When it is significant, block and treatment effects are usually multiplicative (for example a treatment raises yield by a certain percentage rather than a fixed amount), and a log transformation makes the model additive again.",
      r"If the aim is to study the block × treatment interaction (for example when blocks are locations), treatments must be replicated within each block; the design then becomes a generalised RCBD or a multi-location trial."]),
    ("Relative efficiency with a degrees-of-freedom correction",
     [r"The relative efficiency of an RCBD compared with a CRD compares the error variance expected in a CRD with the RCBD's MSE. Because the RCBD gives up \(b-1\) degrees of freedom for blocks, Fisher proposed a correction factor that accounts for the error df of both designs, \(n_1=(b-1)(t-1)\) and \(n_2=t(b-1)\)."]),
    ("Building good blocks",
     [r"Blocks are effective when variation within blocks is small and between blocks is large. In the field, blocks are laid perpendicular to the direction of a fertility or slope gradient, and nearly square when the gradient direction is unknown. In the laboratory or with livestock, blocks can be run times, operators, batches of material, or initial animal weight.",
      r"Blocks should be small. When there are so many treatments that a block becomes large and non-uniform, consider an incomplete block design."])],
   [(r"Why is the ratio MSB/MSE in an RCBD not used as a test of whether blocks are significant?",
     r"Blocks are not randomised and treatment randomisation is restricted within blocks, so there is no randomisation basis for testing blocks. The ratio is only a sign of whether blocking was useful."),
    (r"An RCBD with \(t=4\), \(b=3\) has one missing value. The block total without the missing value is \(B=67\), the treatment total \(T=46\), and the grand total \(G=261\). Estimate the missing value.",
     r"\(\hat{x}=(3\times67+4\times46-261)/(2\times3)=(201+184-261)/6=124/6=20.67\). The error df becomes \(6-1=5\)."),
    (r"The experimental response is a percentage increase, and Tukey's test for nonadditivity is significant. What is the right step?",
     r"Log-transform the response, because multiplicative effects become additive on the log scale. Then repeat the assumption checks."),
    (r"An RCBD gives MSB \(=30\) and MSE \(=6\) with \(b=5\), \(t=6\). Compute the relative efficiency without the df correction.",
     r"\(ER=\frac{4(30)+5(5)(6)}{29(6)}=\frac{120+150}{174}=1.55\). A CRD would need about 55% more replicates."),
    (r"Plots lie on land with a fertility gradient from west to east. What shape and direction should the blocks have?",
     r"Blocks run north–south (perpendicular to the gradient), and the plots within a block are lined up north–south so that all plots in a block have nearly the same fertility.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: a complete RCBD analysis",
                soal="Four varieties (V1–V4) were grown in three blocks. Yields (quintals/plot) are shown in the table. Build the ANOVA, compute the CV and the relative efficiency compared with a CRD.",
                data=[["Block", "V1", "V2", "V3", "V4", "Total"], ["B1", "20", "25", "26", "22", "93"], ["B2", "19", "22", "26", "23", "90"], ["B3", "23", "26", "28", "24", "101"], ["Total", "62", "73", "80", "69", "284"], ["Mean", "20.67", "24.33", "26.67", "23.00", "23.67"]],
                langkah=["Correction factor and total SS (sum of squares of the data 6,800).",
                         "Block SS and treatment SS.",
                         "Error SS with df (3 − 1)(4 − 1) = 6.",
                         "F test for treatments with df 3 and 6.",
                         "CV and relative efficiency (MSB = 8.08).",
                         "Fisher's df correction with n₁ = 6 and n₂ = 8."],
                simpul="Varieties differ highly significantly (P = 0.0017) with a CV of 4.2%. Blocking was very useful: a CRD would need about 2.2 times the replicates for the same precision."))

LE("rancangan-bujur-sangkar-latin",
   [("Randomising a Latin square",
     [r"A Latin square is not randomised by placing treatments one by one, because the condition that each treatment appears once per row and column must still hold. The standard procedure: start from a standard square (first row and column in alphabetical order), then randomise the order of rows, the order of columns, and the mapping of letters to treatments.",
      r"For \(t\ge5\) this procedure does not reach every possible square, but it is adequate in practice. The number of Latin squares grows very fast: 576 for 4×4 and 161,280 for 5×5."]),
    ("Model, expectations, and missing data",
     [r"The model contains additive row, column, and treatment effects. Because each treatment appears once in each row and column, treatment means are automatically adjusted for both. When one observation is missing, its value is estimated with the formula that minimises the SSE, and the error df is reduced by one."]),
    ("Replicated Latin squares",
     [r"A 3×3 or 4×4 Latin square has only 2 or 6 error df, so its tests are weak. The solution is to replicate the square. If rows (or columns) are the same across squares, their effects are pooled; if they differ, rows and columns are nested within squares. The error df increase according to the type of replication."]),
    ("Relative efficiency compared with an RCBD and a CRD",
     [r"After a Latin square has been analysed, we can judge the benefit of controlling both directions. If only rows had been used as blocks (an RCBD), the column variation would have gone into the error; the efficiency formula below compares that expected error variance with the Latin square MSE. A similar formula applies when columns are used as blocks, or when both are ignored (a CRD)."]),
    ("Uses and risks",
     [r"A Latin square suits field experiments with two gradients, greenhouses with light direction and distance from a wall, or experiments with two nuisance factors such as day and operator. In animal or human experiments, rows can be periods and columns individuals, and the design becomes a Latin square crossover; here carry-over effects must be considered.",
      r"The main risk is an interaction among the row, column, and treatment factors, which cannot be tested and goes into the error."])],
   [(r"Why must treatments in a Latin square not be randomised to plots one by one?",
     r"Because free randomisation would almost certainly break the condition that each treatment appears exactly once in each row and column. Randomisation is applied to rows, columns, and the letter mapping of a standard square."),
    (r"How many error df does a 5×5 Latin square have, and how many if two 5×5 squares are replicated with different rows and columns?",
     r"One square: \((5-1)(5-2)=12\). Two squares: \(2(4)(3)+(2-1)(4)=24+4=28\)."),
    (r"A 4×4 Latin square has one missing value. Row total \(R=40\), column total \(C=38\), treatment total \(T=36\), and grand total \(G=170\) (all without the missing value). Estimate it.",
     r"\(\hat{x}=\frac{4(40+38+36)-2(170)}{3\times2}=\frac{456-340}{6}=19.33\)."),
    (r"A 5×5 Latin square gives a column MS \(=12\) and MSE \(=3\). Compute the relative efficiency compared with an RCBD using rows as blocks.",
     r"\(ER=\frac{12+4(3)}{5(3)}=\frac{24}{15}=1.6\). Controlling columns saves about 60% of the replicates compared with a row-blocked RCBD."),
    (r"In a crossover of 4 periods × 4 animals arranged as a Latin square, what is the main risk not handled by the ordinary Latin square model?",
     r"Carry-over of a treatment from the previous period. The ordinary model does not include it; use a Williams balanced Latin square and a model with a carry-over term, or an adequate washout period.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: ANOVA for a 4×4 Latin square",
                soal="Four treatments (A–D) were tested in a 4×4 Latin square in a greenhouse; rows are distance from the wall and columns are light direction. Data and the treatment in each cell are shown in the table.",
                data=[["Row", "C1", "C2", "C3", "C4", "Total"], ["R1", "A 11.5", "B 13.5", "C 13.0", "D 16.0", "54.0"], ["R2", "B 12.0", "C 16.0", "D 17.5", "A 8.5", "54.0"], ["R3", "C 11.5", "D 18.0", "A 8.5", "B 11.0", "49.0"], ["R4", "D 18.0", "A 14.0", "B 14.0", "C 14.5", "60.5"], ["Total", "53.0", "61.5", "53.0", "50.0", "217.5"]],
                langkah=["Treatment totals and correction factor (sum of squares of the data 3,093.75).",
                         "Total, row, and column SS.",
                         "Treatment SS and error SS with df (4 − 1)(4 − 2) = 6.",
                         "F test for treatments with df 3 and 6.",
                         "Relative efficiency (row MS = 5.56; column MS = 6.14)."],
                simpul="Treatments differ highly significantly (P = 0.0004); their means are A 10.6, B 12.6, C 13.8, D 17.4. Controlling two directions of variation was very useful: a CRD would need about three times the replicates for the same precision."))

LE("rancangan-graeco-latin",
   [("Building orthogonal squares for prime t",
     [r"For prime \(t\), mutually orthogonal Latin squares can be built with modular arithmetic. The cell in row \(i\) and column \(j\) (counting from 0) holds \(i+aj\) modulo \(t\), with \(a=1,2,\dots,t-1\). Each value of \(a\) gives one Latin square, and any two squares with different \(a\) are orthogonal.",
      r"So for prime \(t\) (and also prime powers, using Galois field arithmetic) there are \(t-1\) mutually orthogonal squares, the maximum possible. A Graeco-Latin square uses just two of them."]),
    ("Model and analysis",
     [r"The model contains four additive factors: rows, columns, Latin letters, and Greek letters. One of the letters is usually the treatment of interest, while the other three are nuisance factors, or two of them are treatments when the aim is to test two factors without interaction.",
      r"Each factor has \(t-1\) df, so the error has only \((t-1)(t-3)\). For \(t=4\) the error has just 3 df and the test is very weak; replicate the square or use \(t\ge5\)."]),
    ("Hyper-Graeco-Latin designs",
     [r"When three or more mutually orthogonal squares are available, all of them can be superimposed to control more factors. For prime \(t\) there are \(t-1\) squares, so in theory up to \(t+1\) factors (including rows and columns) can be controlled. At that point all df go to factors and none is left for error; in practice leave at least a few df for the error."]),
    ("When to use this design",
     [r"A Graeco-Latin square suits industrial or laboratory experiments with several identifiable nuisance sources, such as raw material (rows), operator (columns), machine (Greek letters), and method (Latin letters). It is rarely used in field experiments because the no-interaction assumption among four factors is hard to meet and the error df are few."])],
   [(r"Why can a 6×6 Graeco-Latin square not be built?",
     r"Euler conjectured and Tarry (1900) proved that no pair of orthogonal 6×6 Latin squares exists. For every other \(t\ge3\) such a pair exists."),
    (r"How many error df do 5×5 and 7×7 Graeco-Latin squares have?",
     r"5×5: \((4)(2)=8\). 7×7: \((6)(4)=24\)."),
    (r"Using \((i+aj)\bmod 5\), write row \(i=2\) for \(a=1\) and \(a=2\) (columns \(j=0\) to 4).",
     r"\(a=1\): 2, 3, 4, 0, 1. \(a=2\): 2, 4, 1, 3, 0."),
    (r"What is the maximum number of mutually orthogonal squares for \(t=7\), and how many factors can be controlled in theory?",
     r"\(t-1=6\) squares, so rows, columns, and 6 letters: 8 factors. All \(49-1=48\) df are used up (8 × 6), leaving no error df."),
    (r"A researcher wants to test 5 methods with raw material, operator, and machine as nuisance factors, each at 5 levels. Which design fits, and how many runs?",
     r"A 5×5 Graeco-Latin square: raw material as rows, operator as columns, machine as Greek letters, method as Latin letters. 25 runs with 8 error df.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second example: building a 5×5 Graeco-Latin square",
                soal="Build a 5×5 Graeco-Latin square with arithmetic modulo 5: Latin letters from a = 1 and Greek letters from a = 2. The numbers 0–4 map to A–E and α–ε.",
                data=[["Row", "C0", "C1", "C2", "C3", "C4"], ["0", "Aα", "Bγ", "Cε", "Dβ", "Eδ"], ["1", "Bβ", "Cδ", "Dα", "Eγ", "Aε"], ["2", "Cγ", "Dε", "Eβ", "Aδ", "Bα"], ["3", "Dδ", "Eα", "Aγ", "Bε", "Cβ"], ["4", "Eε", "Aβ", "Bδ", "Cα", "Dγ"]],
                langkah=["Latin letters: row i, column j holds (i + j) mod 5.",
                         "Greek letters: (i + 2j) mod 5.",
                         "Check orthogonality: each Latin–Greek pair appears exactly once in the 25 cells.",
                         "Randomise the order of rows, columns, and the mapping of letters to treatments before use."],
                simpul="This design controls four sources of variation in 25 runs and leaves 8 error df. The table above is the standard form; randomising rows, columns, and labels is still required."))

LE("blok-tidak-lengkap-bib-lattice",
   [("Intrablock analysis",
     [r"Because not every treatment appears in every block, treatment totals are mixed up with the effects of the blocks they sit in. The intrablock analysis removes block effects using the adjusted total \(Q_i\): the treatment total minus the average of the totals of the blocks containing that treatment. The adjusted treatment SS is computed from \(Q_i\), while the block SS is computed in the ordinary (unadjusted) way, and the error is the remainder.",
      r"The adjusted mean is the grand mean plus the adjusted treatment effect. All differences between adjusted means have the same standard error, because every pair occurs together \(\lambda\) times."]),
    ("Recovery of interblock information",
     [r"Block totals also carry information about treatments, because different blocks contain different treatments. If block effects are treated as random, the interblock information can be combined with the intrablock information, weighted by the inverse of their variances. The result is a more precise estimator, especially when the variation between blocks is not too large.",
      r"Today this combination is done automatically by a mixed model (REML) with blocks as a random effect."]),
    ("Constructing a BIB design",
     [r"Fisher's inequality states that a BIB needs at least as many blocks as treatments, \(b\ge t\). A simple way to build a BIB: take all combinations of \(k\) out of \(t\) treatments (\(b=\binom{t}{k}\)), suitable for small \(t\). Another is cyclic construction: start from an initial block, then add 1 modulo \(t\) repeatedly.",
      r"When no BIB exists for the desired \(t\) and \(k\), use a partially balanced incomplete block design (PBIB) or a Patterson–Williams alpha design, the standard choice for line and variety trials with many entries."]),
    ("Lattices and alpha designs",
     [r"A simple lattice places \(t=k^2\) treatments in a \(k\times k\) grid; the first replicate uses the grid rows as blocks and the second uses the columns. Each replicate is complete (resolvable), so if block effects are small the experiment can be analysed as an RCBD. A balanced lattice uses \(k+1\) replicates and becomes a BIB.",
      r"Alpha designs generalise this idea to any \(t=sk\) and are widely used in plant breeding."])],
   [(r"Check whether \(t=6\), \(k=3\), \(b=10\), \(r=5\) can be a BIB, and find \(\lambda\).",
     r"\(rt=30=bk\) holds. \(\lambda=r(k-1)/(t-1)=5(2)/5=2\), an integer, and \(b=10\ge t=6\). The parameters are consistent (this BIB does exist)."),
    (r"Compute the efficiency factor for \(t=7\), \(k=3\).",
     r"\(E=\frac{t(k-1)}{k(t-1)}=\frac{7(2)}{3(6)}=0.778\)."),
    (r"In a BIB, why is the block SS computed without adjustment while the treatment SS must be adjusted?",
     r"In the intrablock analysis, blocks enter the model first so their SS is taken as is; treatments enter after blocks so their SS must be cleaned of block effects (sequential SS). In complete block designs the two are orthogonal so the order does not matter."),
    (r"How many blocks are needed if a BIB is built from all combinations of 3 out of 5 treatments? What are \(r\) and \(\lambda\)?",
     r"\(b=\binom{5}{3}=10\). \(r=bk/t=30/5=6\), \(\lambda=r(k-1)/(t-1)=6(2)/4=3\)."),
    (r"A trial of 100 lines with blocks of 10 plots. Which design fits?",
     r"A 10×10 lattice (\(t=k^2=100\)) or an alpha design; each replicate is split into 10 incomplete blocks so that field variation within a replicate can be controlled.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: intrablock analysis of a BIB",
                soal="Four treatments were tested in a BIB with t = 4, k = 3, b = 4, r = 3, λ = 2. Data by block are in the table (– means the treatment is not in that block).",
                data=[["Block", "T1", "T2", "T3", "T4", "Total"], ["1", "12", "15", "16", "–", "43"], ["2", "11", "13", "–", "19", "43"], ["3", "10", "–", "15", "17", "42"], ["4", "–", "15", "16", "21", "52"], ["Total T_i", "33", "43", "47", "57", "180"]],
                langkah=["Correction factor, total SS (sum of squares of the data 2,812), and block SS.",
                         "Adjusted totals: the treatment total minus the average total of the blocks containing it.",
                         "Adjusted treatment SS.",
                         "Error SS with df 11 − 3 − 3 = 5 and the F test.",
                         "Adjusted means and the standard error of their differences."],
                simpul="Treatments differ highly significantly. The adjusted means (11.38, 13.88, 15.50, 19.25) differ from the raw means (11.0, 14.3, 15.7, 19.0) because T2 happened to sit often in good blocks and T1 in poorer ones."))

LE("percobaan-faktorial",
   [("Effect notation in a 2^k factorial",
     [r"In a two-level factorial, each combination is written with the lower-case letters of the factors at their high level: (1), a, b, ab, and so on. The main effect of A is the difference between the mean at high A and low A; the AB interaction effect is half the difference between the effect of A at high B and at low B. All effects can be computed from a table of signs: the contrast divided by \(r2^{k-1}\), and the effect SS is the squared contrast divided by \(r2^k\).",
      r"Yates' algorithm computes all contrasts at once with pairwise sums and differences repeated \(k\) times, a fast method once used for hand calculation."]),
    ("Fixed, random, and mixed models",
     [r"When all factors are fixed, every effect is tested against the MSE. When some factors are random, the F denominator changes according to the expected mean squares. In a mixed model with A fixed and B random, the main effect of A is tested against the A×B interaction MS, not the MSE, because the interaction variation enters the expected MS of A.",
      r"A practical example: varieties (fixed) are tested at several randomly chosen locations. The variety effect must be compared with how inconsistent the varieties are across locations (the interaction), not with the error within locations."]),
    ("Unreplicated factorials",
     [r"A \(2^k\) factorial with large \(k\) is often run without replication, so there are no pure error df. The principle of effect sparsity says only a few effects are truly large. Small effects are assumed to be just error; a normal or half-normal plot of the effects shows active effects as points that depart from a straight line.",
      r"Lenth's method gives a formal test: a pseudo standard error (PSE) is computed from the median absolute effect, then each effect is divided by the PSE and compared with a t distribution with \(m/3\) df, where \(m\) is the number of effects."]),
    ("Quantitative factors and interaction",
     [r"When one factor is quantitative (for example dose), its SS and its interaction SS can be split with orthogonal polynomial contrasts. The A × B linear component tests whether the slope of the dose response differs between levels of A; the A × B quadratic component tests whether the curvature differs. This split is far more informative than letters for every combination.",
      r"The same can be done with regression: model the response against dose with a different slope for each level of A (ANCOVA with interaction)."]),
    ("Presenting and interpreting interaction",
     [r"Interaction is shown with an interaction plot: cell means on the vertical axis, levels of one factor on the horizontal axis, and a line for each level of the other factor. Parallel lines mean no interaction; non-parallel lines mean there is one. A quantitative interaction (same direction, different size) still allows careful discussion of main effects, while a qualitative interaction (crossing lines, reversed rankings) requires discussing simple effects."])],
   [(r"In a 2×2 factorial with \(r=2\), the combination totals are (1) = 20, a = 30, b = 24, ab = 42. Compute the effects of A, B, and AB.",
     r"\(A=(30+42-24-20)/(2\times2)=28/4=7\). \(B=(24+42-30-20)/4=4\). \(AB=(42+20-30-24)/4=2\)."),
    (r"With the same data, compute the SS for A.",
     r"Contrast A \(=28\); \(SS_A=28^2/(2\times4)=784/8=98\)."),
    (r"Varieties (fixed) are tested at 5 randomly chosen locations. What is the variety F computed against, and why?",
     r"Variety F \(=MS_V/MS_{V\times L}\), because in a mixed model the expected variety MS contains the variety × location interaction variance. The conclusion then applies to the population of locations, not just these five."),
    (r"An unreplicated \(2^4\) factorial gives 15 effects. The small effects are used by Lenth's method. How many df does the reference t distribution have?",
     r"\(m/3=15/3=5\) df."),
    (r"An interaction plot shows the lines for two varieties crossing: variety 1 is better at low dose, variety 2 at high dose. How should this be interpreted?",
     r"A qualitative interaction: neither variety is best overall. Discuss simple effects, that is, compare varieties at each dose, and recommend a variety according to the dose used.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: a 2×3 factorial with polynomial partitioning",
                soal="Two varieties (a1, a2) and three equally spaced fertiliser doses (b1, b2, b3) were tested in a CRD with r = 3. Cell means (t/ha) and totals are in the table; the total SS is 378.",
                data=[["Variety", "b1", "b2", "b3", "Total"], ["a1", "21 (63)", "26 (78)", "29 (87)", "228"], ["a2", "23 (69)", "28 (84)", "35 (105)", "258"], ["Total", "132", "162", "192", "486"]],
                langkah=["Correction factor, SS for A, SS for B, and SS for combinations.",
                         "Interaction and error SS (combination SS = 366, error df 12).",
                         "F tests: A (df 1), B (df 2), A × B (df 2).",
                         "Split SS for B with linear (−1, 0, 1) and quadratic (1, −2, 1) contrasts on the dose means 22, 27, 32.",
                         "Split the interaction: linear slope a1 = 29 − 21 = 8, a2 = 35 − 23 = 12."],
                simpul="The dose response is linear (all of SS B is linear). The interaction is significant mainly because the slopes differ: yield of a2 rises 6 t/ha per dose step, a1 only 4. Both varieties respond to dose, but a2 is more responsive."))

LE("faktorial-pecahan-fractional-factorial",
   [("Building a fractional factorial from a basic full factorial",
     [r"A \(2^{k-p}\) fraction is built by writing the full factorial for \(k-p\) basic factors, then defining \(p\) added factors as products of the basic columns. Each generator yields one word in the defining relation; the complete relation contains all generators and their products (\(2^p-1\) words in total).",
      r"The aliases of an effect are found by multiplying it by every word in the defining relation, using the rule that any letter squared equals the identity."]),
    ("Confounding to split into blocks",
     [r"The same idea is used to fit a full factorial into small blocks. Choose an unimportant high-order interaction, then place the combinations with a + sign in one block and those with a − sign in another. That interaction can no longer be distinguished from the block effect (it is confounded), but all other effects can still be estimated.",
      r"Partial confounding confounds different interactions in different replicates, so each interaction can still be estimated from the replicates where it is not confounded."]),
    ("Foldover to break aliases",
     [r"When a resolution III design shows several large effects aliased with two-factor interactions, run a second fraction with all signs reversed (a full foldover). The two fractions together form a resolution IV design: main effects are free of two-factor interaction aliases. A foldover on a single factor reverses the signs of that factor only, freeing its main effect and all two-factor interactions involving it.",
      r"A sequential strategy like this (screen first, then clarify) is more economical than running a large design straight away."]),
    ("Plackett–Burman designs and minimum aberration",
     [r"Plackett–Burman gives two-level screening designs with \(N\) runs, a multiple of 4, for up to \(N-1\) factors, for example 11 factors in 12 runs. These designs are resolution III with complex aliasing: each two-factor interaction is spread partially over many main effects.",
      r"When several fractions have the same resolution, choose the one with minimum aberration: the one with the fewest words of the shortest length, so that the fewest low-order interactions are aliased with each other."]),
    ("Analysing unreplicated fractions",
     [r"Because fractions are usually not replicated, the analysis uses a normal plot of effects or Lenth's method, together with the principle of effect hierarchy: main effects are more likely to be large than two-factor interactions, and two-factor interactions more likely than higher-order ones. The heredity principle also helps interpret aliases: an AB interaction is more likely active when A or B is also active."])],
   [(r"For a \(2^{5-1}\) with \(E=ABCD\), what are the defining relation and the resolution?",
     r"\(I=ABCDE\), word length 5, so resolution V: main effects and two-factor interactions are not aliased with each other."),
    (r"In a \(2^{6-2}\) with \(I=ABCE=BCDF=ADEF\), list the aliases of A.",
     r"\(A\cdot ABCE=BCE\), \(A\cdot BCDF=ABCDF\), \(A\cdot ADEF=DEF\). So A is aliased with BCE, DEF, and ABCDF (all third-order or higher)."),
    (r"A \(2^3\) factorial is split into two blocks by confounding ABC. Which combinations go in the same block as (1)?",
     r"Those with the same ABC sign as (1), the − sign: (1), ab, ac, bc. The other block: a, b, c, abc."),
    (r"A resolution III design shows large effects for A and B, but A is aliased with BC and B with AC. What step confirms them?",
     r"Run a full foldover (all signs reversed). The combined fractions have resolution IV, so A and B are freed from two-factor interactions."),
    (r"What is the maximum number of factors that can be screened with a 20-run Plackett–Burman design?",
     r"\(N-1=19\) factors, in a resolution III design.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: estimating effects in a 2^{4−1}",
                soal="A 2^{4−1} fraction with D = ABC was run in standard order of A, B, C. The responses (yield, %) are in the table. Estimate the effects and interpret their aliases.",
                data=[["Run", "A", "B", "C", "D = ABC", "y"], ["1", "−", "−", "−", "−", "50"], ["2", "+", "−", "−", "+", "64"], ["3", "−", "+", "−", "+", "52"], ["4", "+", "+", "−", "−", "70"], ["5", "−", "−", "+", "+", "55"], ["6", "+", "−", "+", "−", "69"], ["7", "−", "+", "+", "−", "58"], ["8", "+", "+", "+", "+", "82"]],
                langkah=["Defining relation and alias structure (resolution IV).",
                         "Effect = contrast divided by N/2 = 4. Effect of A:",
                         "The other effects in the same way.",
                         "Rank the absolute effects and judge them with a normal plot: A stands out, B and C are moderate, the rest are small."],
                simpul="Factor A has the largest effect (+17.5), followed by C (+7) and B (+6); D has almost no effect. The 3.5 effect belongs to AB or CD; by the heredity principle AB is more likely because A and B are active while D is not."))

LE("petak-terbagi-split-plot",
   [("Expected mean squares and the two errors",
     [r"The main plot is the experimental unit for factor A; the subplot is the experimental unit for factor B. So there are two errors: error (a) is the block × A interaction and reflects variation between main plots, while error (b) reflects variation between subplots within main plots. The expected mean squares show the right test pairs.",
      r"Error (a) is usually larger than error (b), because main plots are larger and more variable. As a result A is tested with few df and a large error, so its power is low; that is the price paid for the convenience of applying A to large plots."]),
    ("Standard errors for four kinds of comparison",
     [r"The standard error of a difference depends on the kind of comparison. The first two comparisons use a single error. Comparing two levels of A at the same (or a different) level of B involves both errors, so the standard error is a combination, and the critical t is also a weighted combination (the Cochran–Cox approach) or uses Satterthwaite degrees of freedom."]),
    ("Satterthwaite degrees of freedom",
     [r"The combined standard error is a linear combination of two mean squares, so it does not follow a chi-square distribution exactly. Satterthwaite gives the effective degrees of freedom for such a combination. The method is also used automatically by mixed models (the Kenward–Roger or Satterthwaite approach) for unbalanced data."]),
    ("Split plot in time and mixed models",
     [r"Repeated measurements on the same plots (for example yields in years 1, 2, 3) are often analysed as a split plot in time, with time as the subplot. This analysis assumes equal correlation between times (sphericity); if not, use repeated measures with a correction or a mixed model with a suitable covariance structure.",
      r"A mixed model with random block and block × A effects gives the same results for balanced data and remains valid for unbalanced or missing data."]),
    ("When to choose a split plot",
     [r"Choose a split plot when one factor needs large plots or is hard to change (tillage, irrigation, growth-chamber temperature, sowing date), or when an extra factor is to be inserted into an experiment already under way. Put the factor you most want to study precisely, and its interaction, on the subplots."])],
   [(r"Why is the main-plot factor in a split plot often not significant even when the difference between its means is large?",
     r"Because it is tested against error (a), which is usually large and has few df \((r-1)(a-1)\). The critical F is high and the power is low."),
    (r"A split plot with \(a=3\), \(b=4\), \(r=4\). Compute the df for error (a), error (b), and the total.",
     r"Error (a): \((4-1)(3-1)=6\). Error (b): \(3(4-1)(4-1)=27\). Total: \(48-1=47\)."),
    (r"\(E_a=12\), \(E_b=3\), \(r=3\), \(b=4\). Compute the standard error of the difference between two A means, and between two B means at the same A.",
     r"A means: \(\sqrt{2(12)/12}=1.41\). B at the same A: \(\sqrt{2(3)/3}=1.41\). They happen to be equal here; in general they differ."),
    (r"With the data of the previous question, compute the standard error of the difference between two levels of A at the same level of B.",
     r"\(\sqrt{2[(4-1)3+12]/(3\times4)}=\sqrt{2(21)/12}=\sqrt{3.5}=1.87\)."),
    (r"Irrigation (hard to change) and variety (easy) are studied together. Which factor goes on the main plots?",
     r"Irrigation on the main plots because it needs large plots; variety on the subplots so that the variety effect and the irrigation × variety interaction are tested more precisely.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: a complete split-plot ANOVA",
                soal="Two tillage methods (T1, T2) as main plots and three varieties (V1–V3) as subplots, in three blocks. Yields (quintals/ha) are in the table; the total SS is 390.44.",
                data=[["Block", "T1V1", "T1V2", "T1V3", "T2V1", "T2V2", "T2V3"], ["1", "30", "34", "37", "33", "38", "43"], ["2", "26", "31", "33", "35", "37", "43"], ["3", "31", "37", "38", "31", "38", "43"], ["Mean", "29.0", "34.0", "36.0", "33.0", "37.7", "43.0"]],
                langkah=["Main-plot analysis from the block × T totals (101, 114; 90, 115; 106, 112).",
                         "MS for error (a) with df 2 and the F test for tillage.",
                         "Subplot analysis.",
                         "F tests for variety and interaction with denominator df 8.",
                         "Standard error for comparing two tillage methods at the same variety, with the combined t."],
                simpul="Variety and the interaction are significant, but tillage is not (P = 0.118) even though its means differ by 4.9 quintals, because error (a) is large (CV 11.1%) with only 2 df. The least significant difference between two tillage methods at the same variety is t′ × SE = 4.06 × 1.97 = 8.0; even the largest difference (at V3, 43.0 − 36.0 = 7.0) does not exceed it. To test tillage properly, the experiment needs more blocks."))

LE("petak-terbagi-dua-kali-split-split-plot",
   [("Model and expected mean squares",
     [r"A split-split plot has three nested experimental units: main plots (factor A), subplots (factor B), and sub-subplots (factor C). Each level has its own error, and every effect is tested against the error of the unit level where that effect sits: A against error (a); B and A × B against error (b); C and every interaction containing C against error (c).",
      r"The expected mean squares show this arrangement: error (a) contains all three variance components, error (b) two, and error (c) only one."]),
    ("Standard errors of comparisons",
     [r"As in a split plot, the standard error depends on the kind of comparison. Comparisons that involve only one level use one error; comparisons across levels (for example two levels of A at the same combination of B and C) use a combination of errors with a weighted t′ or Satterthwaite degrees of freedom."]),
    ("Interpreting a three-factor interaction",
     [r"An A × B × C interaction means that a two-factor interaction (for example B × C) differs by level of the third factor. To interpret it, split by the levels of one factor and draw a two-factor interaction plot for each level. If the three-factor interaction is not significant, discuss the significant two-factor interactions; if none is, discuss the main effects.",
      r"With three errors and many sources of variation, the risk of finding spurious effects is considerable. Limit the discussion to effects that matter agronomically or technically, not just those that are statistically significant."]),
    ("Simpler alternatives",
     [r"This design is complex to run and analyse. Where possible, consider a split plot with a B × C factorial on the subplots (only two errors), or a strip-split plot when two factors need strips. For unbalanced data, a mixed model with random block, block × A, and block × A × B effects is the safest approach."])],
   [(r"A split-split plot with \(a=3\), \(b=2\), \(c=4\), \(r=3\). Compute the df for errors (a), (b), and (c).",
     r"Error (a): \((r-1)(a-1)=4\). Error (b): \(a(r-1)(b-1)=6\). Error (c): \(ab(r-1)(c-1)=3(2)(2)(3)=36\)."),
    (r"Which error is the A × C interaction tested against, and why?",
     r"Error (c), because interactions containing C sit at the sub-subplot level, the experimental unit to which C is applied."),
    (r"\(E_c=4\), \(r=3\), \(a=2\), \(b=3\). Compute the standard error of the difference between two C means (averaged over A and B).",
     r"\(\sqrt{2(4)/(3\times2\times3)}=\sqrt{8/18}=0.67\)."),
    (r"How should a significant A × B × C interaction be interpreted?",
     r"A two-factor interaction differs by level of the third factor. Draw B × C interaction plots for each level of A and discuss the pattern at each level."),
    (r"When is a split plot with a B × C factorial on the subplots better than a split-split plot?",
     r"When B and C are equally easy to apply to small units. That design has only two errors, is simpler, and gives B the same precision as C.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second example: standard errors for various comparisons",
                soal="A split-split plot with a = 2 (irrigation), b = 3 (variety), c = 2 (N dose), r = 3 blocks gives E_a = 12, E_b = 6, E_c = 4. Compute the standard error of the difference for various comparisons.",
                data=[["Comparison", "Formula", "SE"], ["Two A means", "√(2E_a/rbc)", "1.15"], ["Two B means", "√(2E_b/rac)", "1.00"], ["Two C means", "√(2E_c/rab)", "0.67"], ["Two B at the same A", "√(2E_b/rc)", "1.41"], ["Two C at the same A", "√(2E_c/rb)", "0.94"], ["Two C at the same A and B", "√(2E_c/r)", "1.63"]],
                langkah=["Degrees of freedom of the three errors.",
                         "An A mean is averaged over r × b × c = 18 observations.",
                         "A C mean within one A and B combination contains only r = 3 observations."],
                simpul="Although E_c is the smallest, comparing C within one combination of A and B has the largest standard error because each mean uses only 3 observations. A standard error always depends both on the error used and on the number of observations behind the mean."))
